  "definition": "Block Propagation Time is the latency metric measuring how long it takes for a newly mined or validated block to be disseminated to all (or a target percentage of) nodes in a blockchain peer-to-peer network after its initial announcement. It is a critical determinant of blockchain security and throughput: long propagation times increase the probability of temporary forks (stale/orphan blocks), waste miner effort, and reduce effective network throughput. Bitcoin historically achieved median propagation times of approximately 1\u20132 seconds to reach 50% of nodes, while Ethereum's uncle mechanism tolerated higher latency by including competing blocks in the canonical chain.",
